<!--
    structsynth nest: a pointer field whose loads are dereferenced at constant
    offsets is typed as a pointer to a record of its own, or to the record that
    holds it.

    Source (gcc -O2 -fcf-protection=none, max-inline-recursive-depth-auto=3,
    the shape of grep's kwset treenext):

        struct inner { long a; int b; int c; };
        struct outer { struct inner *in; long x; int y; };
        struct tree  { struct tree *llink, *rlink; long val; unsigned char label; };

        long use_outer(struct outer *o)                        // @0x100000
        { return o->in->a + o->in->b + o->in->c + o->x + o->y; }

        void treewalk(struct tree const *t, long *next) {      // @0x100020
          if (!t) return;
          treewalk(t->llink, next);
          treewalk(t->rlink, next);
          next[t->label] = t->val;
        }

    use_outer loads o->in once and reads it at 0, 8 and 0xc. treewalk is
    unrolled three levels deep, so a node loaded from llink or rlink is read at
    0, 8, 0x10 and 0x18 with the same types the parameter's own node is.

    With option structsynth param (asserts 1 and 2): the parameter is a
    struct_N, but every read through a loaded field is raw offset arithmetic
    again, (int8)*(int4 *)((int8)v1 + 0xc) and (unsigned long *)*v2.

    With option structsynth nest (asserts 3 to 8): the loads of o->in are
    measured as one record, {0: int8, 8: int4, 0xc: int4}, minted as struct_2,
    and the outer field is struct_2 *. Each node loaded in treewalk holds
    everything the parameter's node holds, so llink and rlink point at the
    parameter's own record: struct_4 holds two struct_4 * members, and every
    level reads v->field_0x18 instead of *(uint1 *)&v[3].
-->
